Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult the current development Record before acting.

The second inquiry block is installed after `@inquiry-foundation-review`. Its exact continuation is `inquiry-design-units`, `met-calibration-design`, `inquiry-finite-probability`, `inquiry-conditional-dependence`, `inquiry-expectation-variation`, `inquiry-randomized-comparisons`, `inquiry-evidence-limits`, `inquiry-design-study`, `inquiry-design-review`. Eight new lessons reuse one existing calibration-design Record, whose original prose, imports and specialist continuations are preserved. A separate preparation note bounds its common reading. The first inquiry study and all previous stock/catalog programs remain unchanged.

The block develops observation/allocation units, sampling versus assignment, nuisance factors and order, finite outcome sets and weights, counting, conditioning, dependence, expectation and variance. A six-unit potential-response model derives unbiasedness from balanced inclusion probabilities without assuming independent assignment indicators. It distinguishes a fixed mathematical target, an estimate averaged over the assignment mechanism and one realized estimate. The interpretation remains an effect on indicated length for six stipulated units, not physical size, accuracy or a population-wide causal claim.

The original study compares twenty complete balanced allocations with eight assignments within three predeclared pairs. Both estimate the stipulated 2 mm effect without bias; their variances are 40/9 and 2/3 mm², respectively. The selected complete-design realization gives a 6 mm contrast. A separate sharp-null calculation holds the observed vector fixed and obtains a tie-inclusive two-sided tail of 2/20=0.1. The lessons explain why that null is stronger than a zero average effect, why the paired assignment set cannot replace the complete design, and why this twenty-allocation two-sided rule cannot reject at 0.05. A finite rank/tie argument proves rejection probability at most the predeclared threshold. Non-rejection is not proof of no effect, and the tail probability is not the probability that a hypothesis is true.

Both `allocation_study.py` and its independently structured `allocation_checks.py` are embedded in the study for reproduction. They ran with **Python 3.14.8** and passed **25,179 exact checks**, including all **4,096 binary potential-outcome tables**, **729 fixed null-outcome tables** and **15,309 threshold comparisons**. Separate mask enumeration and an independent contrast expression check the main program; labeled-card and nonuniform-weight examples check the finite definitions. General results retain algebraic proofs; no physical, educational or laboratory experiment is claimed. Missing observations, interference, deliberate assignment, noncompliance and selective reporting remain explicit limitations.

The existing NIST source preserves its identity and original assignments. Its design sections 5.3.3–5.3.3.2 and critical-value/p-value discussion 7.1.3.1 were inspected; the added note qualifies significance-level wording for finite discrete tests. A new shared source links the Dartmouth-hosted **CHANCE version dated 4 July 2006** of Grinstead and Snell’s Introduction to Probability, with the title-page credit to Peter G. Doyle and its relationship to the 2003 second edition stated accurately. Metadata, rights, contents and selected finite definitions were inspected; broader assignments are distinguished from that bounded inspection. Its author-page request failed, while the institutional PDF was accessible. Continuous/infinite probability, calculus and advanced inference remain later preparation. Source media remain external.

The installed graph/reference/preservation audit passed for **154 selected common Records** (root, six maps and 147 learning Records), exact intended edges and directed reachability, **1,784 discovered documents** from **104 authored entrances**, **29,694 local references** and **2,939 unique explicit Hephaestus identities**. All sixteen historical standalone discovery sets are unchanged. The full parser passed for **2,941 files and 2,979 Records**, six Frequencies, six Rules and twenty-one Extensions; the register passed with 262 Records. Warnings-as-errors `cargo check --locked -p lince-resources --features publication --tests` passed through Nix in session `63967`. Targeted whitespace and shared-content checks passed. The Lince rules scan found no applicable pipe-prefixed requirement; ordinary assertions, source discovery and selection boundaries remain intact. No application behavior changed.

The first integration test now checks the exact twelve-block composition and preserves both allocation programs across export/reopening in addition to the original stock/catalog programs. Representative runtime entrances are the proof source, second physics review and second inquiry review. The other eighteen tests are unchanged. The complete test file compiled with warnings as errors against the existing built publication libraries, using the immutable snapshot as its manifest home; compilation session `69838` passed. This is distinct from runtime execution.

